// The Oodle1 decoder, against streams the real compressor produced.
//
// The corpus cannot be committed: 83,184 files of Nival's copyrighted data. But
// the vectors below can, because nothing of theirs is in them. Each input was
// authored here, chosen for a path through the decoder, and compressed by
// granny2.dll's own GrannyOodle1BeginSimple / Compress / End, so what is
// committed is a mechanical transform of our own bytes. That makes these a real
// test of the decoder against the reference encoder, runnable on a machine that
// has never seen the DLL.
//
// Only the compressed side is committed. The plain side is regenerated below,
// because every input is a rule two lines long, and a rule is easier to check by
// eye than a kilobyte of hex. The rule and the array have to agree, which is why
// each is stated next to the other.
//
// Whether the decoder agrees with the DLL on the *shipped* corpus is a separate
// question, answered separately: 3,000 retail files were decoded through both and
// compared byte for byte. That cannot be a unit test, since it needs the data and
// the DLL, but it is the measurement that matters and it is recorded in the
// commit that added this file.

